ns toward C because reliable decision\u2011making and crew morale are paramount for safe, long\u2011distance flights, and the reported positive atmosphere suggests his teamwork issues may be manageable.\n**Remaining uncertainty**\n- How severe C\u2019s communication difficulties are in practice (e.g., do they lead to misunderstandings with ATC or co\u2011pilots?).\n- Whether C\u2019s egocentric attitude could undermine crew cooperation on extended missions.\n**What would change my vote**\n- Evidence that C\u2019s communication problems have caused or are likely to cause safety\u2011critical errors.\n- Strong evidence that B\u2019s interpersonal style is benign in practice or that D\u2019s leadership can be effectively compensated, making their technical strengths outweigh C\u2019s weaknesses.\nIn summary, given the current evidence, Candidate\u202fC appears to best satisfy the reliability, decision\u2011making, calmness, and crew\u2011morale requirements of a long\u2011distance pilot, pending clarification of his communication and teamwork limitations.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_3\", \"vote\": \"Candidate C\"}", "thoughts": "We have gathered all missing positive attributes for teamwork/communication. Now we need to produce our public message with provisional preference, summarizing evidence, including info from others (Agent 1 gave positive leadership for A, also said C creates positive atmosphere but that was from Agent 1's info; we have that as info from others). Agent 1 also said C creates positive atmosphere (teamwork). Agent 2 gave no positive teamwork for B. Agent 1 gave no positive for D. So we have:\n\nOur own info: A: anticipate dangerous, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, weaknesses: not good at criticism, unorganized. B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good weather assessment, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks. C: quick correct decisions, difficulty communicating, eg